ew months  and then eventually  you know brian you get asked once in  your life to do something that really is  not what you were expecting this door  opens  but it's running the genome project and  you're going to walk away from it  because it's not convenient oh  yeah i mean it's high risk high reward  oh yeah there's a possibility there  if you don't mind my asking  since we just discussed faith earlier on  in facing a decision like that  does faith play a role in your personal  life to figuring out what to do oh yeah  i every time i've had a big decision  i have tried to find a place to be set  aside without distractions  a retreat of some sort  for that decision i spent a  very long day in a little chapel in  chapel hill north carolina where my  daughter was at that point  a uh undergraduate or maybe she was a  medical student by then  just on my knees trying to figure out  what was this a calling or was this  really something that was a distraction  that was not going to turn out well  and at the end of that long day somehow  even song occurred in that chapel which  i hadn't expected  i took part in it and it felt like i  have peace  this is something i should do  and so you you you took the helm of the  human genome project  and i guess it wasn't too long after  that  some competition cropped up  from  from the private sector  uh craig venter and  and and his his approach  and and the question i have for you  there is  was that  competition  driven by  wanting to be first to get this result  or was it i guess the other side of it  was  there was a there was a threat at some  level that the results  could be  patented and kept  from from the public was it a blend of  those two that that drove you on the  competition front  it was more the latter to be honest  craig had been part of the public effort  uh he was funded by the genome project  through the nih for the first few years  and then he stepped aside from that  started this company called solera and  aimed to try to beat the government  effort by getting there first and made  it very clear that he intended uh to  recoup  the investment of his stockholders  by claiming ownership of the dna  sequence our shared inheritance that was  anathema to all of us who worked in the  public project and were giving the  sequence away every 24 hours that was  our goal  and so it very much became not so much a  race about who's got better technology  we're using the same technology  it became a race about whether this is  going to be a commodity or whether this  is going to be in the public domain and  certainly many of us felt very strongly  about that  ultimately i mean he did a credible job  he was using all the public data because  it was public  and adding some of his own to it but the  public data was good enough to generate  a really good version of the human  genome project by the summer of 2000  when there was this announcement that a  draft was in hand and shortly after that  the private sector fold it because there  was no reason for that to continue there  was not going to be a product there that  they could claim it was already out  there in the public domain it was a good  outcome  and so was the competition in your view  and retrospect was it a good thing that  there was competition to sort of  it probably was it got everybody's  adrenaline going  people were working really hard they  worked even harder than to be sure that  this wasn't something that ended up out  of reach for the average member of our  human species  and it got more public attention because  up until the point that there was this  so-called race for the genome most  people didn't even know it was happening  and now all of a sudden oh this guy has  a yacht and that guy rides a motorcycle  and it became people magazine and it got  attention and i  i gather you in the motorcycle  yacht is that  i got the motorcycle yes  and some of it was ridiculous but at  least it got public interest like oh  what is this is this going to make a  difference maybe this is something to  pay attention to and they did  and you you  kind of famously in in describing the  result  blended  your personal faith in terms of a  description i guess you've described the  human genome as as god's instruction  manual for life for some where in that  that combination of work is that how you  do see it i did again not that i saw  that god had sort of reached in here and  written out the ac's g's and t's but as  we talked about earlier that in my view  god the creator  made it possible through the natural  laws that i also think god was behind  god as the greatest physicist the  greatest mathematician for a universe to  come into being that would lead to this  moment where for the first time a big  brain species was able to read their own  instruction book that's pretty  breathtaking  but but you you also do not describe  yourself  as a deist i got you you don't  or maybe in part you do  you know some would say god set  everything up at the get-go receded back  and allowed the laws of physics to cause  the universe to expand  collections of particles to coalesce  into atoms and molecules i mean  ultimately when we talk about the  genetic code  it's four letters three billion letters  long but each of those letters is a  stand-in for a very specific molecule  which is a collection of very specific  atoms held together by very specific  forces you don't think that god's  interceding  in those details is that is that right  no god is much more awesome than that  god put this all in place so that that  would be the inevitable outcome of what  started more than 13 billion years ago  but it's not a theist god who then got  bored and went on to do something else  again coming back to my faith god is  interested in us god cares about good  and evil god actually came to join us  for a few brief years in the person of  jesus that's atheist god if there ever  was one  but why would god  make things  so complicated  i mean as a physicist  the laws of nature i i do see have a  simplicity and elegance of course  but the the richness of the world that  emerges from that  it's a very  complicated process and you could  imagine that if god wanted things to  turn out a particular way there might  have been a simpler approach  does that not suggest to you that  maybe it wasn't that kind of intelligent  designer  behind it all  no i don't see it that way i think what  we see in terms of the incredible  complexity is part of the gift because  complexity also means beauty in many  ways it makes it more interesting sure  if god wanted to create a universe where  everything was clockwork  and if there were any intelligent beings  they were all sort of automatically  wired to worship god from the moment  they were born i guess god could have  done that but it wouldn't have been very  interesting god seems to be interested  in us and giving us that kind of free  will to figure out what we make of all  of this and i think that's a much more  intriguing kind of creation  than something that might have been  simpler  but maybe not so much worth god's time  right  and so with the human genome sequenced  how do you feel about how that data has  been used so far are we are we further  along than you would have hoped by this  stage not as far along  in different areas it may be  one or the other of that i certainly  think in terms of our ability now to be  able to use genomics in research it's  been profound  my own lab studies two diseases one's  type 2 diabetes a common cause  unfortunately of much suffering and the  other a very rare disease progeria which  causes premature aging we couldn't have  done any of that  without the offspring of the human  genome project and the ability to be  able to look at uh dna samples in a  blink of an eye practically and ask  what's there and now to look at that at  a single cell and ask what that cell  what it's doing omics is everywhere  graduate students today in any area of  biology simply cannot imagine how we did  anything useful before we had the genome  project and all of its spin-offs so  research utterly transformed a clinical  practice cancer utterly transformed  cancer is a disease of the genome now  most people in the developed world if  you develop cancer that kind of set of  tools is going to be used to help figure  out which therapy is most likely to help  you and that's a big step forward it's  certainly turning out to be incredibly  valuable in figuring out  all kinds of dilemmas in terms of  diagnoses that people haven't been able  to arrive at for young children where  you know there's something not right now  the really method of choice sequence the  genome see if you can figure out what's  going on there and sometimes save four  or five years of diagnostic odysseys  that lead you almost nowhere  that's all transforming more and more  certainly an infectious disease look  with covit  the spinoff of the genome project to be  able to rapidly sequence viral genomes  has led us to the ability to be able to  track a pandemic in record speed and  discover new variants just as soon as  they start to appear all that's  happening and all of that is incredibly  gratifying has it transformed the  average person's medical care  not really the way i wish it would we  have ways to go particularly when it  comes to prevention that's something  that we really wanted to see that your  individual genome ought to allow a more  precision approach to prevention instead  of one size fits all  this program that we're running called  all of us  in the us which is enrolling a million  people in a prospective longitudinal  study where complete genome sequence and  electronic health records and all kinds  of environmental exposures are being  melded together is going to get us a lot  closer to that and programs like the uk  biobank in britain and programs in  australia that have this same idea we're  going to get there but that takes really  big data sets probably a lot of machine  learning in order to sift through the  complexities of the human body which is  as you said earlier maybe god could have  made this a little simpler but it's what  we got  yeah  uh in the remaining time that we haven't  wanted to turn to the new job that you  mentioned before that you've now taken  on  uh president biden's science advisor  and  with all of the challenges that we face  in the world and all the opportunities  that we have so many of them have a  scientific basis science at their core  from what we're talking now in terms of  medicine personalized medicine alternate  energy issues of climate change global  pandemics of course look nuclear  weapons and war is sort of  back on the table in a shocking way in  recent times  where do you see the role  of someone with your background who  perhaps may have a much closer line to  the ear  of the president of the united states  what role do you see for yourself there  well i didn't expect this role and i'm  right now on my 18th day so i'm still  sorting out exactly how i can best play  this role but we are fortunate in the us  right now to have a president who cares  deeply about science and who is really  interested in getting that kind of  information on every topic hungry to see  the way in which that kind of  science-based evidence-based  decision-making can result in a better  outcome  and so i'm finding myself very much  called upon and it is certainly as you  said it's not just about mice areas that  i'd be more familiar with in biomedicine  it's also about many other aspects where  science is critical um we're having a  meeting day after tomorrow on fusion and  whether there's a advance or a set of  advances that might be possible here uh  to deal with our energy demands and of  course climate crisis uh which  we are way behind on where we should be  in terms of dealing with global warming  and what are the things that can be done  that 